---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const stats = [
  { value: '40', label: 'containers on one board' },
  { value: '45', label: 'Ansible roles, no click-ops' },
  { value: '200+', label: 'alert rules watching it' },
  { value: '1,700+', label: 'automated tests' },
  { value: '61', label: 'tag-gated releases' },
  { value: '76', label: 'incident write-ups' },
];

const stacks = [
  {
    icon: '🎬',
    title: 'Media',
    text: 'A request-to-playback pipeline. Jellyfin serves direct play only, because the Pi cannot transcode. Size limits keep 30 GB disc copies off the disk.',
    tags: ['Jellyfin', 'Sonarr', 'Radarr', 'Prowlarr', 'qBittorrent', 'Jellyseerr', 'Tdarr'],
  },
  {
    icon: '🌐',
    title: 'Network & access',
    text: 'LAN DNS with ad blocking, a reverse proxy for every UI, single sign-on, and remote access over WireGuard. Every host port is scoped to the LAN.',
    tags: ['AdGuard Home', 'Traefik', 'Tailscale', 'Authelia', 'UFW'],
  },
  {
    icon: '📈',
    title: 'Observability',
    text: 'Metrics, logs and alerts for everything above, including jobs that run once a week. Alerts reach a phone through ntfy, rate-limited.',
    tags: ['VictoriaMetrics', 'VictoriaLogs', 'vmalert', 'Alertmanager', 'Grafana', 'Fluent Bit', 'cAdvisor', 'Beszel', 'ntfy'],
  },
  {
    icon: '🧪',
    title: 'Data & ML',
    text: 'A small experiment platform: shared PostgreSQL, S3-compatible storage, experiment tracking, audio labelling, and a speech-recognition backend that pre-annotates it.',
    tags: ['PostgreSQL', 'MinIO', 'MLflow', 'Label Studio', 'ASR backend'],
  },
  {
    icon: '🧰',
    title: 'Platform',
    text: 'The plumbing: a local OCI registry for images built on the Mac, a dashboard, container management and a speed-test history.',
    tags: ['Docker', 'Zot registry', 'Homepage', 'Portainer', 'Watchtower', 'Speedtest Tracker'],
  },
  {
    icon: '🤖',
    title: 'Own runtime',
    text: 'One static Go binary, homelab-agent, holds the decisions that scheduled jobs used to bury in shell: 20 subcommands, 18 tested packages, a checksum-pinned toolchain.',
    tags: ['Go', 'race tests', 'differential fixtures', 'atomic state'],
  },
];

const pipeline = [
  { title: 'Commit', text: 'Conventional commits; pre-commit runs lint, ShellCheck, tests and secret checks.' },
  { title: 'CI', text: 'Python, Bats and Go suites plus ansible-lint on a self-hosted runner.' },
  { title: 'Tag', text: 'A deploy-v* tag decides when to ship. CHANGELOG and TODO move with it.' },
  { title: 'Deep check', text: 'The Pi runs a syntax check, then a --check --diff dry run against itself.' },
  { title: 'Apply', text: 'Only the roles that changed. Host provisioning is never auto-deployed.' },
  { title: 'Roll back', text: 'Any failure reverts to the last good commit and pings the phone.' },
];

const principles = [
  ['Everything is Ansible.', 'Firewall rules, cron jobs, app settings via their APIs: if it is not in a role, it does not exist.'],
  ['Decisions live in tested code.', 'Shell only orchestrates. Diffs, transitions and "should I delete this?" live in Go or Python with tests.'],
  ['Every scheduled job proves it ran.', 'Each cron emits a metric with an alert on it. A job that silently does nothing counts as a failure.'],
  ['One alert per episode.', 'Rules have hysteresis, bulk events are digested under an hourly cap, and routine news waits for a morning report.'],
  ['Write down the class, not just the incident.', 'Every painful failure gets a five-line entry, so the second occurrence triggers the structural fix.'],
];

const lessons = [
  {
    title: 'A dry run that bricked deployments',
    text: "Ansible skips HTTP calls in check mode, so a task reading the skipped call's JSON crashed the deploy controller's safety check, and every release rolled itself back.",
    fix: 'Fix: live-API phases are gated out of check mode, and a test scans every role for the pattern.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Absence is not deletion',
    text: 'When the cloud archive mount dropped, apps saw 759 archived files as deleted, and a library clean-up erased the records for all of them.',
    fix: 'Fix: every destructive job checks an archive health canary first and treats a stale canary as unsafe.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Power faults only show up over time',
    text: 'A second bus-powered SSD passed a load test and two reboots, then vanished on the third with no warning in the logs.',
    fix: 'Fix: a powered hub. Only elapsed time proves a marginal power budget.',
  },
  {
    title: '480 notifications buried the one that mattered',
    text: 'An outage produced one push per affected file. The alert explaining the outage was lost in the flood.',
    fix: 'Fix: producers write to a spool, a digest sends a few messages an hour at most, and routine items go to a daily report.',
  },
  {
    title: '"High CPU" was really a log flood',
    text: 'A stale retry loop wrote about 100 errors a second for 30 hours. The only symptom was a CPU alert flapping at its threshold.',
    fix: 'Fix: a log-rate rule names the flood directly, and CPU alerts hold one episode open instead of flapping.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="One Raspberry Pi, forty containers — a homelab run like production"
  description="How a single Raspberry Pi 4B runs a media stack, observability, single sign-on and a small ML platform, managed with Ansible and released from git tags."
>
  <header class="topbar">
    <div class="wrap">
      <a class="brand" href="/"><img src="/favicon.svg" alt="" />pi-homelab</a>
      <nav class="topnav">
        <a href="#stack">Stack</a>
        <a href="#pipeline">Pipeline</a>
        <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>
        <a href="#lessons">Lessons</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Raspberry Pi 4B · 8 GB RAM · 1.8 TB SSD</p>
        <h1>One small board.<br /><span class="accent">Run like production.</span></h1>
        <p class="lead">
          A credit-card-sized computer runs a home's media, DNS, single sign-on, monitoring and a small
          machine-learning platform, with a laptop helping on the heavy lifting. Nothing was set up by
          hand: every change is code, checked by tests and released from a git tag. The working repository
          is private because it also runs personal services; the public one is a snapshot of its Ansible
          layout and core roles.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-cta">
          <a class="btn primary" href="https://github.com/aemreusta/pi-homelab">Public snapshot →</a>
          <a class="btn" href="#lessons">Read the lessons</a>
        </div>
        <div class="stats">
          {stats.map((s) => (
            <div class="stat"><b>{s.value}</b><span>{s.label}</span></div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="stack">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">What runs on it</p>
        <h2>Forty containers on four ARM cores</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Memory caps are deliberately overcommitted: they contain a runaway rather than reserve RAM,
          and zram swap absorbs bursts. The real budget is watched as working set and swap growth, not
          as the sum of the limits.
        </p>
        <div class="grid">
          {stacks.map((s) => (
            <article class="card">
              <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">{s.icon}</span>
              <h3>{s.title}</h3>
              <p>{s.text}</p>
              <ul class="tags">{s.tags.map((t) => <li>{t}</li>)}</ul>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="pipeline">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">How changes ship</p>
        <h2>GitOps, with a seatbelt</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          A push to <code>master</code> only runs CI. A controller on the Pi deploys the commit the newest
          <code>deploy-v*</code> tag points at, applies just the roles that changed, and rolls the whole
          release back if anything fails.
        </p>
        <ol class="flow flow-wrap">
          {pipeline.map((p) => (
            <li><b>{p.title}</b><span>{p.text}</span></li>
          ))}
        </ol>
        <div class="split">
          <div>
            <h3>House rules</h3>
            <ul class="checklist">
              {principles.map(([head, body]) => (
                <li><b>{head}</b> {body}</li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="diagram mono" aria-label="Release flow">
            <div class="box"><b>git push</b> → CI (pytest · bats · go test · ansible-lint)</div>
            <div class="arrow">↓ tag deploy-v2026.10.20.1</div>
            <div class="box"><b>Pi controller</b> → syntax check → --check --diff</div>
            <div class="arrow">↓ changed roles only</div>
            <div class="box"><b>apply</b> → verify → last-good marker</div>
            <div class="arrow">↓ on failure</div>
            <div class="box"><b>rollback</b> → held state → phone alert</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="architecture">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Doing more with less</p>
        <h2>The Pi orchestrates; others do the heavy work</h2>
        <div class="split">
          <div>
            <h3>A laptop as a compute node</h3>
            <p class="section-intro">
              Video transcoding runs on a MacBook's hardware HEVC encoder through a native Tdarr node,
              and so do preview and sprite generation, all reading the Pi's disk over NFS. The Pi only
              coordinates. A transcode is kept only when its length still matches the source, and files
              typically shrink by more than half. Because the laptop sleeps and travels, presence
              detection quiets the alerts that depend on it, and its workers retry on their own.
            </p>
            <h3>A cloud archive at the original paths</h3>
            <p class="section-intro">
              Cold files move to cloud storage behind an rclone mount, and a mergerfs union keeps them at
              their original paths, so no app notices. The design is fail-safe: if the cloud side
              disappears, local files stay intact and archived titles are merely absent. A local copy is
              deleted only after a checksum match. Uploads go through <b>homelab-rclone</b>, a Google Drive
              client used only by this server for its owner's Drive; see the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="diagram mono" aria-label="Storage and compute layout">
            <div class="box"><b>Raspberry Pi 4B</b><br />Docker · Ansible target · NFS server<br />1.8 TB SSD, one filesystem (hardlinks)</div>
            <div class="arrow">⇅ NFS</div>
            <div class="box"><b>MacBook (when home)</b><br />Tdarr node · HEVC via VideoToolbox<br />preview &amp; sprite workers</div>
            <div class="arrow">⇅ rclone + mergerfs</div>
            <div class="box"><b>Cloud archive</b><br />read-only branch of the library<br />absent ≠ deleted</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="lessons">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Lessons the Pi taught</p>
        <h2>Small hardware, real incidents</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Each of these has an incident write-up and a structural fix in the working repository; most
          also have a regression test.
        </p>
        <div class="lessons">
          {lessons.map((l) => (
            <article class="lesson">
              <h3>{l.title}</h3>
              <p>{l.text}</p>
              <p class="fix">{l.fix}</p>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
